1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,140
 So now we're going to do a bit of a deep dive into the machine language of our CDC 6504 emulator.

2
00:00:06,140 --> 00:00:15,180
 I would suggest that if you're doing extensive work or even if you have some visual limitations that you look at the online documentation.

3
00:00:15,180 --> 00:00:24,980
 It's complete and it's simple and to some degree this lecture is really derived from the online documentation of the CDC 6504.

4
00:00:24,980 --> 00:00:28,780
 And so we always are going to start and talk about the register and just review it.

5
00:00:28,780 --> 00:00:32,240
 Hopefully, after a couple of lectures, it'll be sort of natural to you.

6
00:00:32,240 --> 00:00:37,540
 The program counter is the what next instruction memory starts at zero.

7
00:00:37,540 --> 00:00:40,540
 The accumulator is the best register.

8
00:00:40,540 --> 00:00:51,300
 So it's the CDC 6504 is an accumulator based computer, which means that you can do more things with the accumulator than other registers.

9
00:00:51,300 --> 00:00:57,840
 There are flags, the Z and the N and there's actually more in the 6502, but Z and the N is all I put in here.

10
00:00:57,840 --> 00:01:04,300
 Z says when you're fiddling with registers, was the last result zero or was the last result negative?

11
00:01:04,300 --> 00:01:09,340
 And these are used to control ifs and jumps and loops, the Z and the N.

12
00:01:09,340 --> 00:01:11,540
 The X and the Y are index registers.

13
00:01:11,540 --> 00:01:18,100
 They're just a little less capable of the accumulator register, and they're actually used in index loads, as we'll see.

14
00:01:18,100 --> 00:01:21,740
 Instruction memory is shown in base two for easy decoding.

15
00:01:21,740 --> 00:01:29,840
 Now, the data memory, I show that in hex because mostly what we're putting in the data memory is strings and ASCII characters.

16
00:01:29,840 --> 00:01:41,400
 OK, so the index instructions are good for loops and the X and Y registers are often used to control counted loops or actually look things up within an array.

17
00:01:41,400 --> 00:01:51,500
 And so we have an opcode to clear the X register, clear the Y register, add one to it, add one to X and Y and then subtract one from X and Y.

18
00:01:51,500 --> 00:01:59,600
 Now, if you think about it, the CPU that we built clears and increments with two instructions.

19
00:01:59,600 --> 00:02:04,940
 So we had an instruction of one which increments and zero which clears.

20
00:02:04,940 --> 00:02:09,840
 So in addition to 8-bit instructions, there are 16-bit instructions.

21
00:02:09,840 --> 00:02:17,460
 And so in 16-bit instructions, there is usually a numeric value or a constant or an address that is in the immediately following.

22
00:02:17,460 --> 00:02:21,900
 So that means that the program counter in this case goes up by two.

23
00:02:21,900 --> 00:02:26,940
 And so the load A instruction can take a constant value.

24
00:02:26,940 --> 00:02:31,460
 So you can load A, the character asterisk, which is 42.

25
00:02:31,460 --> 00:02:40,740
 And so you see the load A instruction at memory location zero and then the actual 42 value, in this case base two, is instruction one.

26
00:02:40,740 --> 00:02:50,140
 Or you can basically say we're going to do a load, but the address that we want to load from is taken from the following 8-bits.

27
00:02:50,140 --> 00:02:56,840
 In this case, we're going to load from memory location two and the two is stored in instruction memory location three.

28
00:02:56,840 --> 00:02:58,660
 So we call these immediate.

29
00:02:58,660 --> 00:03:05,700
 And the reason we kind of call these things immediate is the constant or the address is immediately following the instruction in case.

30
00:03:05,700 --> 00:03:08,440
 And so these turn into 16-bit instructions.

31
00:03:08,440 --> 00:03:15,420
 You can see it as you load one instruction in and there will probably be both a current instruction register and an immediate register.

32
00:03:15,420 --> 00:03:21,380
 And you would notice that this instruction that we just loaded has an immediate.

33
00:03:21,380 --> 00:03:27,220
 And so we'd load both of those things and then have them in the CPU to work.

34
00:03:27,220 --> 00:03:31,340
 So loading and storing operations are important.

35
00:03:31,340 --> 00:03:40,020
 So you can load into the A register, the X register, and the Y register, and you can store into the A register, X register, and Y register.

36
00:03:40,020 --> 00:03:53,660
 And you can either load a constant like set A to zero, load A, hashtag zero, or you can say go get memory location F3 and load that in.

37
00:03:53,660 --> 00:03:56,900
 And so those are the instructions that do loading and storing.

38
00:03:56,900 --> 00:04:01,900
 Now, when we start dealing with the arrays, we can use the X and the Y.

39
00:04:01,900 --> 00:04:07,740
 So we can say this address is kind of the beginning of the array and then X and Y are the offset in the array.

40
00:04:07,740 --> 00:04:11,280
 So you start seeing that you can set X to zero.

41
00:04:11,280 --> 00:04:21,180
 You can load something from a starting point in memory plus X and then you can add one to zero and then load something again from that same place in memory plus X.

42
00:04:21,180 --> 00:04:29,540
 And then X become and Y are really well designed to use as iteration variables.

43
00:04:29,540 --> 00:04:35,700
 And then this is where the accumulator is important, where the accumulator is the register that can do the most things.

44
00:04:35,700 --> 00:04:44,300
 So we can't add arbitrary values, so we can't add we can add one to X and Y registers, but we can't add five.

45
00:04:44,300 --> 00:04:48,180
 So ADC hashtag five adds five to it.

46
00:04:48,180 --> 00:04:57,780
 So the same for subtraction and that you can either add an immediate value like a constant or you can go into some memory and grab that memory and add it to the accumulator.

47
00:04:57,780 --> 00:05:03,420
 So you can see it says ACC equals ACC plus memory bracket address.

48
00:05:03,420 --> 00:05:12,100
 That means that we're adding in the data memory at the position that's address, but the address it's an immediate instruction and the next one is the address that we're going to add.

49
00:05:12,100 --> 00:05:16,020
 So adding and subtracting is the accumulator.

50
00:05:16,020 --> 00:05:26,780
 Now, the everything that is adding, subtracting, loading, storing, whatever is doing to whatever register sets the Z and the N flags.

51
00:05:26,780 --> 00:05:33,740
 And so it just as part of the final putting the value into the register, it sets Z and N.

52
00:05:33,740 --> 00:05:38,940
 So if the value we put in to the register was zero, the Z flag will be a one.

53
00:05:38,940 --> 00:05:43,980
 It'll be set. If the value we put in was 42, the Z flag will be zero.

54
00:05:43,980 --> 00:05:50,540
 And the same with positive and negative. Negative numbers are numbers that have the first bit, the tie bit one.

55
00:05:50,540 --> 00:05:53,900
 We're not doing too much. We won't be doing too much with negative in this class.

56
00:05:53,900 --> 00:06:00,340
 We'll mostly be setting the zero, but you can do like less than or equal with the negative.

57
00:06:00,340 --> 00:06:06,900
 So combinations of Z and N can do greater than or equal, less than or equal, et cetera, et cetera.

58
00:06:06,900 --> 00:06:16,980
 And so there is a special instruction called the compare instruction that says, hey, let's check to see if the accumulator equals five.

59
00:06:16,980 --> 00:06:20,740
 But you're really saying five accumulator, check them.

60
00:06:20,740 --> 00:06:25,860
 It's kind of like a subtraction without actually doing the subtraction and then set subtract,

61
00:06:25,860 --> 00:06:31,460
 set the Z and N flags from the result of the subtraction and then throw away the result of the subtraction.

62
00:06:31,460 --> 00:06:41,140
 So that's what the comparison operators are. OK. And so you can compare the accumulator, the X and the Y.

63
00:06:41,140 --> 00:06:46,780
 So there's no if statement in machine code. All there are are conditional jumps.

64
00:06:46,780 --> 00:06:53,660
 And so that you have to get used to it. When I started in Fortran, everything was a go to before we had structured ifs, if then else.

65
00:06:53,660 --> 00:06:59,260
 That that's not in machine code. There is a conditional jump, which we'll talk about.

66
00:06:59,260 --> 00:07:08,900
 And the way you get the conditional jumps to activate is every instruction that changes a register and the compare instructions with don't change registers, set the result flags.

67
00:07:08,900 --> 00:07:16,220
 There's a Z, which is was the result zero or not. And then the N is was the result negative or not.

68
00:07:16,220 --> 00:07:20,420
 And so if the result is zero, the Z is one and the N is zero, because zero is not negative.

69
00:07:20,420 --> 00:07:25,540
 If the result is positive, Z is zero because it's not zero and N is zero because it's not negative.

70
00:07:25,540 --> 00:07:30,740
 And if the result is negative, Z is zero and one is negative.

71
00:07:30,740 --> 00:07:37,540
 And yes, you can use these together to create greater than, greater than or equal to, equal, less than, less than or equal to.

72
00:07:37,540 --> 00:07:47,980
 So that's kind of what you're thinking about, right, is how to implement what we think of as the traditional logical operators in a high level language.

73
00:07:47,980 --> 00:07:55,420
 So the closest we have to an if statement is a compare and then a conditional jump.

74
00:07:55,420 --> 00:08:00,500
 And so there are four instructions, right.

75
00:08:00,500 --> 00:08:02,940
 There's more of them, but these are the four that matter.

76
00:08:02,940 --> 00:08:07,300
 The B, E, Q, branch, if equal, that means the Z flag is set.

77
00:08:07,300 --> 00:08:11,900
 B, N, E, branch, if not equal means the Z flag is clear.

78
00:08:11,900 --> 00:08:17,340
 And then branch minus B, M, I, and then B, P, L means branch plus.

79
00:08:17,340 --> 00:08:22,620
 So, for example, here's a little tiny bit of code that we can do an uppercase.

80
00:08:22,620 --> 00:08:26,780
 We're going to convert a lowercase to uppercase and leave uppercase alone.

81
00:08:26,780 --> 00:08:35,260
 OK, so we do a L, D, A, hashtag, quote, P, quote, which is the ASCII value per P.

82
00:08:35,260 --> 00:08:38,820
 Now, normally we'd read this from input, but we're not going to.

83
00:08:38,820 --> 00:08:42,380
 We're just going to hard code the very the value we're going to convert.

84
00:08:42,380 --> 00:08:47,420
 Then we're going to compare that to the ASCII value for A.

85
00:08:47,420 --> 00:08:51,900
 So that comparison then sets the Z and the N.

86
00:08:51,900 --> 00:08:59,180
 And then we're going to say BMI to location 08, which we'll talk about this in a second,

87
00:08:59,180 --> 00:09:02,540
 how that works, how you know that that's 08.

88
00:09:02,540 --> 00:09:07,580
 That's kind of tricky, but that's an address in instruction memory, the 08.

89
00:09:07,580 --> 00:09:14,660
 And then if it's minus, we will skip the next instruction, but if it's not minus,

90
00:09:14,660 --> 00:09:18,980
 we're going to subtract 20, a hex 20, because that's converting to a lowercase.

91
00:09:18,980 --> 00:09:24,020
 If you look at the ASCII table, you see that the difference between an uppercase A and

92
00:09:24,020 --> 00:09:32,340
 a lowercase A is 20, the lowercase A is 20 hex higher than an uppercase A.

93
00:09:32,340 --> 00:09:38,700
 And then that BMI 08, if it's already uppercase, meaning it's a lower number, that jumps to

94
00:09:38,700 --> 00:09:40,500
 the BRK and the program stops.

95
00:09:40,500 --> 00:09:47,740
 You can think of this as if the accumulator is less than the letter A, we're going to

96
00:09:47,740 --> 00:09:49,900
 skip the next instruction.

97
00:09:49,900 --> 00:09:55,180
 And if we don't skip, we're going to subtract 20 hex.

98
00:09:55,180 --> 00:09:56,820
 And if we do skip, we're done.

99
00:09:56,820 --> 00:10:02,100
 And then in the accumulator will be the uppercase letter, whether or not the original letter

100
00:10:02,100 --> 00:10:07,980
 that was loaded into the accumulator was uppercase or lowercase.

101
00:10:07,980 --> 00:10:11,300
 And so this is where flow charts come back.

102
00:10:11,300 --> 00:10:16,260
 Remember I said flow charts are not cool, although if you ask AI, often flow charts

103
00:10:16,260 --> 00:10:17,260
 are cool.

104
00:10:17,260 --> 00:10:18,500
 They're very retro.

105
00:10:18,500 --> 00:10:26,220
 Well, the key to flow charts is that they work exactly like assembly and machine language.

106
00:10:26,220 --> 00:10:31,980
 So if we look at the first instruction, which is loading the accumulator with the letter,

107
00:10:31,980 --> 00:10:35,500
 the lowercase P, then we're doing a compare instruction.

108
00:10:35,500 --> 00:10:38,980
 So the compare instruction is not something we think of in a high level language.

109
00:10:38,980 --> 00:10:40,660
 We're asking a question.

110
00:10:40,660 --> 00:10:45,980
 We're like, huh, compare the accumulator to A and set the flags.

111
00:10:45,980 --> 00:10:52,700
 That's what the compare instruction is doing, is accumulator equal to less than or greater

112
00:10:52,700 --> 00:10:54,980
 than A?

113
00:10:54,980 --> 00:11:02,260
 And so then we say, was the result of that particular compare, a BMI, negative?

114
00:11:02,260 --> 00:11:05,540
 Because BMI means is the N flag set?

115
00:11:05,540 --> 00:11:13,100
 And if the N flag is set, we're going to jump to instruction memory location 08.

116
00:11:13,100 --> 00:11:15,420
 If not, we're going to fall through.

117
00:11:15,420 --> 00:11:24,460
 And by the way, the actual $1.08 is in the immediately following instruction memory.

118
00:11:24,460 --> 00:11:28,580
 And so where to jump is the 08.

119
00:11:28,580 --> 00:11:36,240
 Then if we don't jump, we're going to subtract 20 hex, and the 20 is also in immediate memory.

120
00:11:36,240 --> 00:11:44,340
 And then the place the program stops is at instruction memory 0x08.

121
00:11:44,340 --> 00:11:51,900
 So the way, if you're doing this by hand, you would actually kind of leave instruction

122
00:11:51,900 --> 00:11:59,340
 memory 05 blank, lay everything else out, and then you see where 0x08, the halt, works.

123
00:11:59,340 --> 00:12:06,780
 And then you would go copy that back in to the 0x05 memory location.

124
00:12:06,780 --> 00:12:11,740
 That seems painful, painful even for eight instructions.

125
00:12:11,740 --> 00:12:15,660
 Thankfully, we have this thing called assembly language.

126
00:12:15,660 --> 00:12:18,940
 So there's no need to compute these instructions by hand.

127
00:12:18,940 --> 00:12:25,140
 And so if you look at the BMI in this code, you say BMI skip, and then right before the

128
00:12:25,140 --> 00:12:28,220
 instruction that you want to skip to, you put a little label with a colon.

129
00:12:28,220 --> 00:12:29,220
 So skip colon.

130
00:12:29,220 --> 00:12:35,300
 Now it turns out that just like I told you on the last slide, the assembler is making

131
00:12:35,300 --> 00:12:36,740
 two passes.

132
00:12:36,740 --> 00:12:43,140
 It literally is assembling all the code and remembering where it needs to change addresses.

133
00:12:43,140 --> 00:12:47,080
 So it kind of keeps track of that's going to wherever skip turns out to be because skip

134
00:12:47,080 --> 00:12:48,500
 could be later.

135
00:12:48,500 --> 00:12:51,700
 So it has to do it all, has to lay it all out, has to figure out where it's going to

136
00:12:51,700 --> 00:12:57,980
 go in the memory, and then it has to go back and replace the skip with the eight hex so

137
00:12:57,980 --> 00:12:59,420
 that it knows what it's doing.

138
00:12:59,420 --> 00:13:00,980
 But for us, we don't care.

139
00:13:00,980 --> 00:13:02,220
 We write assembly language.

140
00:13:02,220 --> 00:13:09,500
 We use labels, and the whole notion that you've got to do two passes by hand, we just write

141
00:13:09,500 --> 00:13:11,220
 assembly language.

142
00:13:11,220 --> 00:13:18,620
 And so our larger example codes will be assembly language.

143
00:13:18,620 --> 00:13:23,380
 And so the jumps, like I said, are far simpler than if logic.

144
00:13:23,380 --> 00:13:29,260
 So in addition to the BEQ, the branch equal, branch not equal, branch minus, and branch

145
00:13:29,260 --> 00:13:33,180
 plus, we also have an unconditional jump.

146
00:13:33,180 --> 00:13:36,900
 That's ignoring, you know, you jump to an address.

147
00:13:36,900 --> 00:13:41,740
 That just says don't look at the flags, just unconditionally jump.

148
00:13:41,740 --> 00:13:44,460
 But just think about this for a second.

149
00:13:44,460 --> 00:13:49,720
 The PC, the program counter, is itself a register.

150
00:13:49,720 --> 00:13:58,500
 So even though we have these opcodes that say branch, sure, or jump, in a sense, you

151
00:13:58,500 --> 00:14:02,860
 could think of these as just a set of the PC.

152
00:14:02,860 --> 00:14:09,160
 If we had a assembly language that said set PC to this address, then that would mean that

153
00:14:09,160 --> 00:14:14,140
 the PC, the next time it would retrieve an instruction, would go to where it's at.

154
00:14:14,140 --> 00:14:19,940
 So you can think of a jump as set the PC to this address.

155
00:14:19,940 --> 00:14:25,740
 Or a jump conditional says if this condition is true, set the PC to this address.

156
00:14:25,740 --> 00:14:29,740
 And of course, these are all 16-bit instructions.

157
00:14:29,740 --> 00:14:38,000
 And so that address is in the immediately following memory location, OK?

158
00:14:38,000 --> 00:14:43,980
 So there are also no for and while loops in machine language.

159
00:14:43,980 --> 00:14:49,980
 And so we have to construct them with these comparisons and conditional branching.

160
00:14:49,980 --> 00:14:54,260
 So I'm not going to bother showing you the machine language for this.

161
00:14:54,260 --> 00:14:56,660
 The emulator will allow you to see that.

162
00:14:56,660 --> 00:14:58,300
 I'm just going to show you the assembly.

163
00:14:58,300 --> 00:15:01,500
 So on the left is assembly language.

164
00:15:01,500 --> 00:15:07,740
 An assembly language is a textual language that is not zeros and ones, but there is a

165
00:15:07,740 --> 00:15:14,420
 one-to-one correspondence that every single assembly language statement turns into directly

166
00:15:14,420 --> 00:15:15,420
 into machine language.

167
00:15:15,420 --> 00:15:19,560
 Now it might be an 8-bit machine language or a 16-bit machine language.

168
00:15:19,560 --> 00:15:25,140
 And the assembler, which is a program that converts this text to machine language, the

169
00:15:25,140 --> 00:15:30,260
 assembler knows all the rules, the syntax of the pound sign 5.

170
00:15:30,260 --> 00:15:34,940
 So really, while we have been talking about machine language, we mostly been talking about

171
00:15:34,940 --> 00:15:35,980
 assembly language.

172
00:15:35,980 --> 00:15:38,340
 So let's take a look at a really simple for loop.

173
00:15:38,340 --> 00:15:42,660
 Now this for loop doesn't do anything, it's just constructing this for loop.

174
00:15:42,660 --> 00:15:45,120
 Or a while loop, it's a top-tested loop.

175
00:15:45,120 --> 00:15:49,660
 So if we go through this, the first thing we do, and I mentioned that x registers are

176
00:15:49,660 --> 00:15:52,100
 good for loops and arrays.

177
00:15:52,100 --> 00:15:56,160
 First thing we're going to do is we're going to clear the x register.

178
00:15:56,160 --> 00:16:00,680
 Then we're going to have a label that says, here's a place we're going to come back to.

179
00:16:00,680 --> 00:16:04,180
 But that doesn't take up any memory, that just is something that the assembler is going

180
00:16:04,180 --> 00:16:07,900
 to remember that location, wherever that location is.

181
00:16:07,900 --> 00:16:13,240
 Then we're going to compare the x register to 5, like that's saying, huh, hey, let's

182
00:16:13,240 --> 00:16:17,580
 just look at x, let's look at 5, and then set the z and the n flags.

183
00:16:17,580 --> 00:16:22,080
 And beq to end, end is a label in this case.

184
00:16:22,080 --> 00:16:28,700
 So if x equals 5, we're going to jump to the end label.

185
00:16:28,700 --> 00:16:33,780
 If we're not jumping, we're coming through, we're going to add 1 to x, which is the INX

186
00:16:33,780 --> 00:16:37,140
 instruction, or x equals x plus 1.

187
00:16:37,140 --> 00:16:42,820
 Now on this right-hand side, these x's are not really just any old variable.

188
00:16:42,820 --> 00:16:44,620
 That means the x register.

189
00:16:44,620 --> 00:16:50,980
 So there's only like the accumulator and the x and the y and program counter and instruction

190
00:16:50,980 --> 00:16:51,980
 register.

191
00:16:51,980 --> 00:16:52,980
 But these are not variables.

192
00:16:52,980 --> 00:16:55,960
 These are just plain old registers.

193
00:16:55,960 --> 00:17:02,300
 So after the INX instruction, we do an unconditional jump, and you can see that on the flow chart

194
00:17:02,300 --> 00:17:07,080
 on the right-hand side, perfectly represented to jump back to loop.

195
00:17:07,080 --> 00:17:08,440
 Now where is loop?

196
00:17:08,440 --> 00:17:10,020
 What number is loop?

197
00:17:10,020 --> 00:17:12,060
 We don't know, and we don't care.

198
00:17:12,060 --> 00:17:16,740
 But the assembler is going to turn that before it gets loaded into the memory's machine language.

199
00:17:16,740 --> 00:17:20,420
 Loop becomes a number, just like end becomes a number.

200
00:17:20,420 --> 00:17:25,180
 So we jump to the top of the loop, and you can see that we added 1 to x, and then we

201
00:17:25,180 --> 00:17:26,180
 compare it to x.

202
00:17:26,180 --> 00:17:32,660
 We check the branch, which in the second time around, it goes straight down and adds 1 again.

203
00:17:32,660 --> 00:17:38,180
 So you can see this is a for loop that's going to go round and round and round until x is

204
00:17:38,180 --> 00:17:42,340
 equal to 5, and then it's going to jump to end.

205
00:17:42,340 --> 00:17:45,360
 And at end, the program is going to halt.

206
00:17:45,360 --> 00:17:50,860
 In the next section, we're going to go into-- we're not going to worry quite so much about

207
00:17:50,860 --> 00:17:52,460
 the machine language.

208
00:17:52,460 --> 00:17:55,020
 We're going to worry more about the assembly language.

209
00:17:55,020 --> 00:18:01,740
